    "q-131725": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131725",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-y=3 \\\\  & -4x+(k-5)y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k-5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & k-5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=3 \\\\ (k-7)y=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
